/**
 * Shipped Advice Coves: how to shop well, as opposed to what to buy.
 *
 * The counterpart to `shop-coves.php`, and seeded the same way — by a
 * migration, from this file, matched on (market, slug), refreshed only while
 * `editorial_source` still says `seed`.
 *
 * ## Where this came from, and what survived
 *
 * Three WordPress sites — bstore.be, bstore.nl and webprice.eu — carry roughly
 * 280 pages of Amazon and online-shopping writing accumulated since 2016. This
 * file is what is left after reading them: eight subjects that are still worth
 * publishing, rewritten from scratch against sources checked in August 2026.
 *
 * **Nothing here is ported text.** Not one sentence, and the reason is not
 * fastidiousness — it is that the old text was substantially wrong. The
 * consumer-rights article, the single most linked page across the three sites,
 * was last edited in 2019 and said refunds take 30 days (it is 14), that the
 * withdrawal period extends by three months when a trader fails to inform you
 * (it is twelve), and it said both of those in the same page as the correct
 * figures. It also served **identical text to Belgian and Dutch readers**,
 * which is the mistake worth dwelling on: the Dutch legal guarantee has no
 * fixed term at all, so telling a Dutch reader they get "two years" talks them
 * out of a claim they still have on a four-year-old washing machine.
 *
 * That is why these are keyed by **market rather than by language**. `be-nl`
 * and `nl-nl` read the same Dutch and live under different consumer law, and
 * the whole point of `App\Enums\Market` is that we are able to say so.
 *
 * ## What may and may not be said here
 *
 * The same rules `shop-coves.php` follows, for the same reason, plus one:
 *
 * - **No prices, fees, delivery times or subscription costs.** They change
 *   without notice and a reader who acts on a wrong one is out of pocket. This
 *   is why there is no "is Prime worth it" article despite three of them in the
 *   sources: every version was a list of prices, and every version was stale
 *   within a year of being written.
 * - **No "cheapest", "best" or "fastest".** The comparison answers that per
 *   product.
 * - **A legal claim carries its date.** Every rule stated here is one whose
 *   commencement can be cited — 28 May 2022 for the price-reduction rule, 1
 *   June 2022 for the Belgian presumption, 13 December 2024 for the GPSR, 1
 *   July 2026 for the customs change. A rule with a date can be re-checked by
 *   the next person; "recently the EU decided" cannot.
 *
 * ## Amazon links
 *
 * Amazon is named throughout, because it is the shop the reader checks next
 * whatever we do. The **first substantive mention in each article** carries an
 * `[[amazon:TERM]]` token, which {@see CoveMarkup} resolves
 * to that market's storefront with that market's Associates tag.
 *
 * Every later mention is plain text on purpose. A paid link on every occurrence
 * of a word turns an article about how to avoid being misled into something
 * that is itself trying to move you, and the reader can tell.
 *
 * In `en` the token degrades to its label and no link appears, because no tag
 * has been issued for `amazon.co.uk` under this account. That is not a gap in
 * this file to be filled in later — it is the tag table doing its job. Issue a
 * tag and these articles start linking with no edit here.
 *
 * ## Structure
 *
 *   topic key => market => [slug, title, blurb, body, meta_description, faq]
 *
 * `es` is absent throughout. It is an unpublished market with no supply, and an
